                <pb facs="0800" n="702" sameAs="frus:frus1930v02/pg702"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">839.00 Revolutions/18: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in the Dominican Republic</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Curtis</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Acting Secretary of
                            State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Santo Domingo</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1930-02-26T11:00:00-04:40">February 26, 1930—11
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received 2:45 p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>20. The British Chargé d’Affaires informs me that a small British warship will
                    arrive here within 20 hours.</p>
                <pb facs="0801" n="703" xml:id="pg703"/>
                <p>The commander of the revolutionary forces and the Government civil authorities
                    are showing apparently sincere desire to maintain order but there are great
                    numbers of idle, irresponsible persons in the city and the situation remains
                    delicate. Trujillo is still holding the fortress nominally for the
                    Government.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Curtis</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
